```

Names are proposed. Preserve current framework conventions and test placement where equivalent files already exist. Do not create every later-phase file as an empty stub. The SDK determines its supported routing integration; verify its pinned version rather than copying an old Next.js tutorial.

Transport guards verify and route. Shared application operations decide authorization. Repositories persist and enforce constraints. Admin-web must not query authorization tables directly as an unrestricted database client. Browser-only code must not import server tokens, database credentials or provider adapters.

## 10. Exact proposed endpoint contract

These are new application design endpoints, not routes that already exist. Only the existing `GET /health/live` is reported implemented. Do not implement supplier endpoints in this module.

| Phase | Endpoint | Behavior / required permission |
|---|---|---|
| Existing | `GET /health/live` | Minimal public process liveness; no business or credential information |
| 3B | `GET /v1/staff/me` | Verified pre-enrolled ACTIVE staff; `staff.self.read`; return own identity/memberships only |
| 3B | `GET /v1/staff/tenants/:tenantId/access` | Own active membership and effective tenant permissions; no commercial data |
| 3C | `GET /auth/login` | BFF starts supported hosted login with fixed redirect rules |
| 3C | `GET /auth/callback` | SDK validates authorization response, state, nonce and code exchange; create server session |
| 3C | `POST /auth/logout` | CSRF-protected local session termination plus documented provider logout/revocation behavior |
| 3D | `GET /v1/tenants/:tenantId/staff` | `tenant.staff.read`; tenant-scoped minimal staff directory |
| 3D | `POST /v1/tenants/:tenantId/access-requests` | `tenant.access.request`; allowed low-risk changes only |
| 3D | `POST /v1/platform/access-requests` | `platform.access.request`; submit a controlled access/invitation/reactivation request |
| 3D | `POST /v1/platform/access-requests/:id/approvals` | Appropriate approval permission, distinct eligible approver, fresh authentication and exact request version |
| 3D | `POST /v1/platform/staff/:id/suspend` | `platform.staff.suspend`; immediate local denial with reason and audit |
| 3D | `POST /v1/tenants/:tenantId/staff/:id/suspend` | `tenant.staff.suspend`; low-risk permitted targets only |
| 3D | `POST /v1/platform/staff/:id/revoke-sessions` | `platform.sessions.revoke`; local cutoff/session invalidation and tracked provider follow-up |
| 3D | `GET /v1/platform/audit-events` | `platform.audit.read`; sanitized security evidence only |
| 3D | `GET /v1/tenants/:tenantId/audit-events` | `tenant.audit.read`; only that tenant's authorized evidence |

The login route names are proposed application contracts; map them to the selected SDK's supported routes. A POST logout wrapper may be needed. Do not create a second custom OAuth implementation to force those paths.

Invitation issuance and redemption require a separate verified contract in 3D: an approved request creates a single-use provider-supported invitation; acceptance binds the verified authenticated identity to the intended invite and existing staff record. Do not add an unauthenticated "set role" or unrestricted "activate account" endpoint.

## 11. Audit, observability and recovery

Record login outcomes, invitation/activation, grant requests, approvals, denials, suspension, reactivation, session revocation, MFA recovery, administrative overrides and bootstrap. A protected state change and its authoritative audit record commit together.

Include actor, target, tenant/scope, action, result, reason, correlation ID, time and approved request version. Never store passwords, bearer tokens, cookie values, MFA secrets, recovery codes, API keys, voucher PINs or whole authentication headers in audit records. Failed authentication has no verified actor; do not label an unverified claimed subject as trusted.

Use append-only application privileges and a separately protected log export before production. A normal admin must not erase evidence. Preserve recovery information and test restorations. Rate-limit and bound logs so attacker-generated failures cannot fill storage unchecked.

The first release must test suspension even while the identity provider is unavailable. Reactivation needs independently approved recovery; it is not the reverse of an emergency one-click suspension.

Provider accounts remain separate: the supplied Qore3 SDK describes assigned TerminalId/password credentials for terminals (pages 7-8). They are not staff login credentials and must never be exposed through staff profile APIs.

## 12. Implementation phases and acceptance

This blueprint refines Brick 3 into bounded substeps. Do not skip or rename Brick 2B, and do not start Brick 4 within this work.

| Substep | Deliverable | Completion evidence |
|---|---|---|
| 3A | Provider-independent staff/membership schema, fixed permission policy, read repositories and audit foundation | Real PostgreSQL migrations, constraint/role/isolation tests; pure permission tests; existing regressions pass |
| 3B | Real issuer-backed token validation and staff-context endpoints | Approved actual non-production issuer/audience/client; real provider token acceptance; negative signature/issuer/audience/expiry/profile tests; local membership denial |
| 3C | Minimal browser staff console with hosted login and server-side sessions | Real browser login/MFA/logout, CSRF, cookie, timeout, no-token-leakage and suspension tests |
| 3D | Invitations, staff access changes, independent approvals and suspension/recovery | Approval replay/race/change tests; lifecycle and external-operation recovery tests; no self-escalation |
| 3E | Security review and operational handover | Key rotation, identity outage, restore/revocation/recovery drills; verified independent evidence; no unresolved critical controls |
| Brick 4 | Controlled publications and authorized catalogue API | Separate future scope |

3A can be implemented without Auth0 credentials. It is local authorization foundation work, not proof of authentication. Keep its application operations internal and test them with explicitly marked synthetic identities. Test identity inputs must not become a deployed HTTP login bypass.

3B is blocked only where actual provider configuration/approval is missing. Complete safe independent work and report missing facts; never mark live identity integration complete using local signing fixtures alone.

### Required tests by boundary

| Boundary | Must prove |
|---|---|
| Identity | Wrong signature/issuer/audience, expired and malformed tokens, ID tokens and machine tokens are rejected |
| Issuer key source | Only configured destinations; controlled key rotation/cache behavior; unknown keys fail closed |
| Membership | Unknown, suspended, revoked or cross-tenant membership denies access |
| Permissions | No role inheritance, wildcard, missing-metadata allow or token-claim-only escalation |
| Accounts | Separate identities for the same person cannot bypass self-approval rules |
| Database | Exact constraints, non-owner grants, scoped reads, rollback, and no pooled-context leakage |
| Revocation | Committed suspension/role removal blocks new protected requests despite an unexpired token |
| Browser | CSRF, redirects, cookies, session fixation/expiry and token leakage are tested |
| Approvals | Request edit invalidates approval; replay does not reapply; requester/beneficiary cannot approve; lost approver rights block execution |
| Recovery | Factor reset cannot bypass strong authentication and permissions; outage does not reactivate suspended users |
| Regression | Existing API, database, application, pricing, synthetic import, Python and structure checks still pass |

## 13. Configuration plan

Existing DB_MIGRATION_URL, DB_RUNTIME_URL and DB_RUNTIME_ROLE remain existing configuration names from the project reports. Preserve actual importer configuration discovered in the repository; do not invent its current name.

New proposed names, introduced only when their substep needs them:

```text
IDENTITY_ISSUER_URL
IDENTITY_API_AUDIENCE
IDENTITY_ALLOWED_CLIENT_IDS
IDENTITY_DB_READ_URL
IDENTITY_DB_WRITE_URL
STAFF_WEB_BASE_URL
STAFF_OIDC_CLIENT_ID
STAFF_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET
STAFF_SESSION_SECRET
STAFF_SESSION_ENCRYPTION_KEY_REFERENCE
```

These are application configuration proposals, not automatically the environment names used by the selected SDK. Add an explicit mapping or use the SDK's documented names consistently. Do not create two conflicting configuration sources. No values are supplied here.

The verifier obtains trusted discovery/JWKS configuration from the approved issuer, not request data. Fail startup for missing required active-module configuration. Never supply fallback secrets. Keep development/test/production identity services and credentials separate.

## 14. Release limitations

No zero-breach guarantee is possible. This design makes security controls explicit and testable; it does not certify the platform or replace independent assessment.

No payment/transfer/refund execution permission is created in Brick 3. Future financial workflows need their own authorization, ledger, idempotency, settlement, recovery and compliance acceptance.

Do not automatically provision a paid service or upload live staff/customer information while provider region, procurement and data-handling conditions remain unresolved. Record actual owner decisions; do not invent an approval date or configured provider account.

## 15. Reference sources

This design combines project-specific proposals with current primary technical guidance reviewed on 10 November 2026. Numeric timeouts and role choices are project proposals, not quoted vendor requirements.

